        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p>OBJECTIVES: <br />
There is a predictable relationship between the amount of sodium in the diet and excretion of calcium in the urine.  It is not known whether the same relationship between sodium and calcium occurs in space or during bed rest. Urinary calcium increases during bed rest and space flight, a phenomenon that is thought to reflect increased resorption of bone and not the amount of dietary salt. We recently observed no increase in the excretion of urinary calcium during a 30-day bed rest study when subjects were fed sodium-restricted diets. The purpose of this research was to compare this 30-day study with restricted dietary salt to a 7-day bed rest study of volunteers fed normal salt diets.</p>
<p>APPROACH: <br />
There were 8 men, aged 30-50 years in the 7-day and 11 in the 30-day study. The 30 day study was carried out 4 years before the 7 day study. Daily dietary sodium averaged 193 mm in the 7-day and 114 mmol in the 30-day study. Dietary calcium was the same at 20 mmol/d. Circulating levels of parathyroid hormone as well as indicators of the hormone action on kidney and bone were evaluated. The excretion of urinary calcium, sodium, and creatinine was monitored carefully. The renal excretion of urinary cyclic adenosine-monophosphate, an indicator of the action of parathyroid hormone on the kidney was measured. The excretion of hydroxyproline, a product of collagen metabolism in bone excreted as a reflection of the level of bone resorption in the urine, was also measured.</p>
<p>RESULTS: <br />
Within the first 3 bed rest days, subjects fed normal sodium diets showed increases in fractional excretion of calcium, increased serum ionized calcium and decreases in circulating parathyroid hormone. In contrast, subjects fed restricted salt diets showed no early increase in ionized calcium or decrease in parathyroid hormone. Urinary hydroxyproline increased during the first few days of bed rest in subjects fed normal salt diets and remained elevated for one week, the duration of the study. This product of collagen metabolism did not increase in the 8 subjects in whom it was measured in the salt restricted study. We concluded that the level of dietary salt has a significant effect on the excretion of urinary calcium and on bone resorption during bed rest. The results suggest that low normal (restricted) dietary salt may reduce the early increase in urinary calcium during bed rest and possibly also, space flight.<end></end></p>
        </div>

